\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Fixed labels can select a different marginal}
\date{2026-10-29}

\p{A global relabeling need not preserve a marginal requested on fixed
labels. In the [four-cycle](fdip-000F), take #{t=m=1} and retain
#{R=\{0,2\}}. Its effective precision is
##{S_{\mathrm{opp}}=
\begin{pmatrix}7/3&-2/3\\-2/3&7/3\end{pmatrix}.}
Exchange labels #{1,2}, fixing #{0,3}, and form #{Q'=PQP^T}.
If the new retained labels are still #{\{0,2\}}, they select the
original adjacent variables #{\{0,1\}}. In the old coordinate order
#{(0,1)\mid(2,3)}, the relevant blocks are
##{\begin{aligned}
A=C&=\begin{pmatrix}3&-1\\-1&3\end{pmatrix},\\
H&=\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\-1&0\end{pmatrix},\\
C^{-1}=HC^{-1}H^T
 &=\frac18\begin{pmatrix}3&1\\1&3\end{pmatrix}.
\end{aligned}}
Subtraction gives
##{S_{\mathrm{adj}}=
\begin{pmatrix}21/8&-9/8\\-9/8&21/8\end{pmatrix}.}
The diagonal entries #{7/3} and #{21/8} differ, so no two-coordinate
permutation conjugates these precisions. There is also no map on the old
retained fields factoring #{\pi_RP} through #{\pi_R}: the fields
#{0} and #{\delta_1} have the same old projection, but their projections
after #{P} are #{0} and #{(0,1)}. Here #{\delta_1} is the
[delta field](fdip-001F) at vertex #{1}, not the edge label #{e_1}.
Transporting the retained subset to
#{p(R)=\{0,1\}} in the new labels restores the square and the
[marginal transport theorem](fdip-000H).}

\p{The blocks are a direct calculation from the
[alternating-cycle precision](fdip-000F), using the
[Schur convention](fdip-000D) of
\citet{Appendix A.5.5, pp. 650-651}{boyd2004convex} after exchanging
its first pivot to #{C}. The example separates a fixed set of labels
from the moving retained space required by \ref{fdip-003E}.}
